  "description": "Train multiclass softmax classification weights using cross-entropy loss gradients and one-hot encoded ground truth.\n\nSoftmax regression generalizes binary logistic regression to multiclass settings using the Boltzmann distribution.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nP(y=k|x) = \\frac{e^{w_k^T x}}{\\sum_j e^{w_j^T x}}, \\quad \\nabla_W \\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{N} X^T (P - Y)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute stable class probabilities via softmax with row-wise max subtraction\n2. Convert target labels into one-hot binary indicator matrix Y\n3. Compute gradient (1/N) X^T (P - Y) and update weights via gradient descent\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with fitted 'weights' matrix and 'train_accuracy'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Softmax Regression\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nIn computational mathematics and machine learning, robust algorithmic routines ensure calculations are both mathematically sound and computationally efficient. **Softmax Regression** implements a core computational building block.\n\nSoftmax regression generalizes binary logistic regression to multiclass settings using the Boltzmann distribution. In this problem, the goal is to: train multiclass softmax classification weights using cross-entropy loss gradients and one-hot encoded ground truth.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nP(y=k|x) = \\frac{e^{w_k^T x}}{\\sum_j e^{w_j^T x}}, \\quad \\nabla_W \\mathcal{L} = \\frac{1}{N} X^T (P - Y)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute stable class probabilities via softmax with row-wise max subtraction\n2. **Phase 2**: Convert target labels into one-hot binary indicator matrix Y\n3. **Phase 3**: Compute gradient (1/N) X^T (P - Y) and update weights via gradient descent\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Algorithmic Complexity**: Designing routines with optimal time and space complexity ensures they scale gracefully to large datasets.\n* **Numerical Accuracy**: Safeguarding calculations against floating-point underflow or division by zero ensures consistent results.\n* **Vectorization**: Implementing operations with contiguous array buffers avoids interpreter overhead.",
